WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.937
Unit 1 is the rule file.

2
00:00:01.937 --> 00:00:07.159
It follows the example shape on Microsoft
Learn's page for creating a detection

3
00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:11.905
rule: the type, a short client-chosen I
D, a display name, the status, the query,

4
00:00:11.905 --> 00:00:15.306
a schedule, and the alert
template, with its severity,

5
00:00:15.306 --> 00:00:18.075
entity mappings and the attack technique.

6
00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:22.605
The guide's example looks for
encoded PowerShell launched by Word,

7
00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:24.500
Excel or Outlook, every hour.

8
00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:25.725
Four rules apply.

9
00:00:25.725 --> 00:00:29.575
The file starts with status
disabled, until its test passes.

10
00:00:29.575 --> 00:00:32.271
Automated actions such
as isolate are left out,

11
00:00:32.271 --> 00:00:35.175
because containment stays
on the response ladder.

12
00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:40.425
Enabling a rule is a change to the status
in the file, reviewed and redeployed.

13
00:00:40.425 --> 00:00:43.400
And rollback sets the
status back to disabled.

14
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:48.936
Learn says deploying needs the Custom
Detection dot Read Write dot All permission,

15
00:00:48.936 --> 00:00:52.306
and a role the A P I accepts:
Detection tuning, manage,

16
00:00:52.306 --> 00:00:56.638
in unified role-based access
control, or Security Administrator.

17
00:00:56.638 --> 00:01:01.750
The Graph security A P I's quota is
ten new rules a minute per application.
